WEBVTT


00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.750
If I said to you,
'The weather is terrible today,'

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.116
what would you expect to see
when you looked out the window?

00:00:07.116 --> 00:00:09.660
Um ... fog?

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.090
Hail?

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.180
Drizzle?

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.530
A tornado?

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.120
You wouldn't know,
would you?

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.670
I was far too vague.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.970
Not only are you none the wiser,
I have almost no control

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:24.120
over what you imagine.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.850
But if I said
'It's pouring with rain out there,'

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:31.170
I've put a much more exact image
in your head.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.920
You know what you're going to see
beyond the curtains.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.320
I might even say
something more exact,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.430
like,
'It's rained 30 inches in the last 24 hours.'

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.540
Or
'The house is now entirely submerged in water.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:44.860
We have to move.'

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.290
The same thing applies
when you're writing.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.530
It's easy to fall into
the trap of vagueness,

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.920
but if you want to make an impact
with your writing,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.760
you need to be specific.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.650
Being vague means your reader has

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.940
to interpret or guess at the details
or what it all means.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.480
And you know what?

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.610
They might not even bother.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.710
You have to make the view clear.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.700
So don't write simply,
'Spending on stationery

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.510
has increased recently.'

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.690
An increase could mean 1% or 1,000%.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.390
It's so vague
it's almost meaningless.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.310
But write,
'Spending on stationery

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.070
doubled in the last year,'
and it's clear to everyone.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.190
And look out for words
that seem to promise significance

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.970
but actually leave your reader guessing.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:35.900
When you use these words,
make sure you fill in the gaps.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.930
It could lead you to say
something truly significant.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.150
Something like,
'Spending on stationery

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.070
doubled in the last year.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.750
That increase is enough
to finally hire a new sales

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:46.970
manager.'

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.620
So always think about the view you're

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.866
creating for your reader,
and make

00:01:50.866 --> 00:01:52.240
sure you've given them everything

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.160
they need to see exactly what you want them to.

